eading Cox by about 27 points", "Prediction markets price Moore's win odds at 97-98 percent"], "rationale": "Maryland's 15-point Democratic lean, Moore's incumbency and overwhelming financial advantage, and consistent polling showing a roughly 27-point lead over Dan Cox leave little doubt about the outcome; every race rater calls it solidly or safely Democratic and markets agree at about 97 percent.", "watch": "A major Moore misstep in the October debate or a late-breaking scandal could narrow the margin, though the fundamentals make an upset very unlikely.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Wes Moore (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Wes Moore, a Democrat, seeks a second term in a state that leans 15 points Democratic, facing Republican Dan Cox. Recent polls from UMBC and Zenith Research show Moore leading by roughly 27 points, and every major race rater calls the contest solidly or safely Democratic. Prediction markets put Moore's win chances near 97 percent.\n\nMoney tells a similar story: Moore has raised about $15.5 million and holds $6.5 million on hand, while Cox has raised roughly $242,000. Cox has run a low-profile campaign with little outside support, and third-party candidates register only marginally in polling. The main question is the size of the margin rather than the winner, with the only debate in early October offering Cox a rare chance to shift the race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 19.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9967, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9939, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9968, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9958, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9956, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9959, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9942, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9961, "margin": 21.0}, {"d